  <p><strong>Why does a seam pop on stretch fabric, and what should I check on a sample?</strong></p>

  <h2>Find the point with the highest strain</h2>
  <p>Start with the crotch, waistband join and any seam crossing a high stretch area. Mark the problem location on the sample and record the movement and size that produced it. If only one size fails, check grading and fit before changing the construction across the entire run.</p>

  <h2>Ask for a matched comparison</h2>
  <p>Have the factory prepare two samples using the same fabric and pattern but differing in one variable, such as thread or seam construction. Check both under the same conditions. For repeatable tests, specify the method, extension, cycles and acceptance criteria with your supplier or lab.</p>

  <h2>Check the revised sample after care</h2>
  <p>Wash and dry according to the intended care instructions under the agreed sample review. Compare seam appearance and function before and after. Keep the approved garment and record the construction or thread revision before production.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.fenalt.com/activewear-manufacturing">Fenalt includes seam elongation among technical production checks buyers can discuss.</a></p>
